<div class="summary">
    <p>
        A solar thermal panel prototype that converts roughly 60% of incident energy into useful heat, and the
        software that made it possible to build in a year: a materials simulator, on-device telemetry, and a
        feedback loop that adjusts flow rate on its own.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>Why it exists</h2>
<p>
    Virginia Tech had design teams for a lot of things, but nothing student-led working on solar-thermal energy.
    In August 2025 I started one. There was no team, no design, and no precedent for the process, which meant
    recruiting an interdisciplinary group, setting the technical direction, and figuring out how a student team
    gets a thermal system built without a lab of its own.
</p>
<p>
    The original design target was a non-concentrated solar thermal panel of about one kilowatt driving an
    Organic Rankine Cycle, buildable for under five thousand dollars. The first phase was a literature review
    covering heat transfer fluids, phase change materials, and small turbines, and it shaped everything that
    followed.
</p>

<h2>What we built</h2>

<h3>The simulator</h3>
<p>
    The first real decision was refusing to fabricate first. Testing coating and absorber-plate materials
    physically would have meant building and measuring six coating-material combinations, a semester we did
    not have. So I built a simulator to evaluate material effectiveness computationally, and we only fabricated
    what the simulation favored. That one decision is what fit the project into a year.
</p>

<h3>The panel</h3>
<p>
    The team designed and built the panel itself. Under test it converted roughly 60% of incident energy into
    useful heat, which is the number the year is measured by.
</p>

<h3>Telemetry and the feedback loop</h3>
<p>
    I integrated a telemetry system with on-device data logging so the panel could run long, unattended tests.
    Then I closed the loop: rather than having someone watch the readings, the system uses live data to make
    micro-adjustments to flow rate on its own, both to hold test conditions steady and to protect the onboard
    electronics from thermal damage. The word that matters is <em>independently</em>. It operates without a
    human in the loop, and that instinct carried straight into the agent work I did later at Finnimo.
</p>

<h2>How the year ran</h2>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Background research.</strong> What had already been tried in solar-thermal with an Organic Rankine Cycle, and what a student team could realistically build.</li>
    <li><strong>Design and build.</strong> Requirements from the research, the simulator to narrow materials, then fabrication of the prototype.</li>
    <li><strong>Experiments.</strong> Instrumented tests on the built panel, with the telemetry loop running the long ones.</li>
    <li><strong>Write-up and pitch.</strong> Documenting the results and pitching the idea beyond campus.</li>
</ol>

<h2>People</h2>
<p>
    Founded and led by me, with an interdisciplinary team of Virginia Tech students across computer science and
    engineering. Advised by Dr. Jiangtao Cheng, Professor at Virginia Tech, whose research spans fluid mechanics,
    heat transfer, and sustainable energy.
</p>

